#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153341 :tutorial/content "A **unit rate** is a ratio of two quantities stated as some amount of one quantity per unit of the other quantity.\n\nFor example, suppose that Carl can run $100$ meters in $20$ seconds. The unit rate is how many meters Carl can run in $1$ second. To compute Carl's unit rate, we divide the number of meters by the number of seconds:\n\n$$\n100 \\div 20 = 5\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $5$ meters per second. In other words, Carl can run $5$ meters in $1$ second.\n\nUnit rates do not always come out to whole numbers. For example, suppose Jamie can run $100$ meters in $15$ seconds. To compute Jamie's unit rate, we again divide the number of meters by the number of seconds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n100 \\div 15 &= \\frac{100}{15} \\\\\n&= \\frac{100 \\div 5}{15 \\div 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{3} = 6 \\, \\textrm{R} 2 = 6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jamie can run\n\n$$\n6 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nmeters per second." :tutorial/id #uuid "9f08d42f-43a3-595e-92b3-d634f656ce2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3073 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
